"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 8.5 and the surgical finding of positive margins. The pathological stage was determined as pT2c with no extraprostatic extension documented. A countervailing finding is the absence of seminal vesicle invasion and negative lymphovascular invasion. Crucially, no lymph nodes were removed, meaning nodal status cannot be assessed, which widens uncertainty regarding risk stratification.\n\nThe biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison is limited as the Gleason pattern and ISUP report from the initial biopsy are missing. Prognostic revision would significantly benefit from postoperative PSA measurements and tracking its subsequent trajectory. The lack of documented follow-up information limits current prognostic certainty. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 37.67–87.91 months; its coverage has not been validated."
